er all  that was really privileged insider  information that Rick got but when Sasha  killed herself in that casket and then  jumped out at negan he was really more  curious over how she killed herself than  by the fact that she turned despite not  getting bitten seems like he just kind  of figured that out from experience  really negan I think just sees this as a  new form of war  they're inspired by Eugene's proposal to  catapult Walker parts over the walls  something I think we all need to see at  some point cuz it's awesome  Meghan just wants to try a form of  chemical warfare in psychological  warfare rather than killing your enemy  outright you make them sick and cause a  panic that destabilizes them from the  inside out that's the idea behind the  various real-world nerve gases that were  developed in the 20th century and why  many of them were classified as inhumane  in the Geneva Accords Meghan believes  that merging their current danger  manpower and firepower with that of the  walkers German power is the key to  bringing Rick to heel and without  spoiling anything coming up yeah this  plan by negan is taken directly from the  comics this she won 22 so this is  actually important to the narrative guys  it's not just our random thing added to  the show but ultimately I think getting  that scientific confirmation from dr.  Jenner and the CDC early on instilled  Rick and his crew with a sort of  reckless dirty bravery that might  explain why they survive this long but  more on that later for now let's move on  to some of the other ways this episode  reminded us of past episodes in our  segment callbacks and speaking of  negan's Walker gut plan this final  close-up of Lucille covered in Walker  blood as he tells us man  look that parallels the moment in his  infamous line up with Rick's crew when  he said look at this look at my dirty  girl for negan it's like he's returning  to that feeling of pride when he could  actually still shock and scare it into  submission  another connection I saw was the radio  broadcaster Gabriel and Harlan stumbled  upon Harlan reads this script that the  guy had for anyone who can hear my voice  know that you're not alone please hold  on we can make it through this if we  don't give up if we keep leaving etc etc  remember earlier in the series Rick used  to broadcast similar messages over  emergency channels in the radio hoping  to connect with any other survivors out  there even though Harlan dismisses it  Gabriel interprets repeatedly sending a  positive message out into the airwaves  and not expecting a response as an  analogy for prayer and he's embracing  that optimism that season one Rick still  had the reality  that this Brady Oh broadcaster killed  himself and the fact that this storyline  really ended with Gabriel back in  custody with Harlan dead is a really  grim reminder of the Bleak place this  show has come to it also makes you  wonder why we even watched this multiple  episodes subplot I guess the writers  want us to think with Sadiq now on the  show two doctors is just too many for  the zombie apocalypse and Gabriel's  foolish for believing in the higher  power now that said I am very interested  in Gabriel's booty hole blindness and I  have some more thoughts on that later  another parallel was Henreid a creepy  Kingdom kid staring down the savior pows  at the hilltop after he killed Gavin we  talked about how that episode painted  Henry has a parallel figure to Carl back  in season 3 when he shot that Woodbury  kid Carl ended up taking the high road  in his life but the fear now is that  Henry is taking the dark path this  debate between Morgan and Carol is  overseen by Maggie who later learns that  Carl died by helping a stranger  her decision to show benevolence and  mercy to the prisoners echoes the way  her father Hershel reacted to Carl back  in that moment he saw that young  reckless inhumanity as a warning sign  for all of their souls and his daughter  Maggie is both inspired by Carl's mercy  and concerned with Henry's wrath moving  her to now share the rations with the  prisoners and if we're looking at past  episodes guys we've got to talk about  Tara this episode and I'm sorry Tara  sucks her ongoing stubbornness and  refusal to accept Dwight switching sides  in this war just completely hypocritical  considering yeah she did the same  freakin thing she fought for the  governor she stood right next to him  when he chopped off Maggie's dad's head  now sure she didn't fire on Rick or kill  anyone the way Dwight did but  considering how grateful she was when  Rick and Maggie let her stay with them  Tara should have some sympathy for a  convert also can we just say it's a  little weird to see Tara mourn Denise  for this long considering they were only  together for like one or two episodes I  think we saw them one scene  of them even talking from what I  remember look I know we were all sad  when Denis died Tara but it's time to  move on Rick's son just died two  episodes ago and Rick is already asking  who's coral anyway Thank You Daryl for  yelling in Tara's face here maybe throw  a walker at her while you're at it okay  let's move on to a few cultural  influences that showed up this episode  and our segment under the influence and  first Gabriel in Harlan's storyline  notice what car they're driving it's a  green Jaguar it's a little odd that in  this zombie apocalypse these fugitives  are driving such a luxury car with a  pretty good shine no less but maybe it's  just because it's a reference another  famous zombies story Shaun of the Dead  Simon Pegg and Nick Frost ik bill Nikes  green Jaguar yeah I guess we'll have to  take the Jag the walking dead crew has  been known to make similar little nods  to pass zombie movies so that wouldn't  surprise me if this is an intentional  easter egg there's also this piggy bank  with the I heart ham radio on the side  you gotta love that pun it ends up  containing keys and a map exactly